{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And he seated.</b> The word את is like the essence of the thing, just as a person says אוהבי (my beloved), where the yod is a sign of the speaker, or one says אוהב אותי (loves me), where the meaning is the essence of the thing itself. Therefore if one says that Reuben killed את Simeon, then Simeon is killed; but if one says Reuben killed Simeon, the meaning is the same — like ורב יעבוד צעיר (Genesis 25:23), אשר ברא אלהים אדם (Deuteronomy 4:32). And do not think that the word את is a sign of the object, because it is found likewise in the plural, and it is found with the agent of the action: ובא הארי ואת הדוב (1 Samuel 17:34) and others likewise. R. Judah the grammarian said that because of the word את he said ויושב (singular) and did not say ויושבו. In my opinion, he said ויושב because Moses is the principal — likewise ותדבר מרים ואהרן (Numbers 12:1): she was the principal of the speech."], :book :exodus, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 10, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Piotrkow, 1907-1911" "https://www.nli.org.il/he/books/NNL_ALEPH990020973480205171/NLI"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T18:33:10.964249276Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}